Here are some suggestions: (some less sensible than others=3D-)

- Hobbits, kobolds and other small-headed monsters (both polymorphed
players and NPCs) could (W)ear the skull as a helmet. Not bad AC, but
tends to shatter when struck. Better than nothing in a scrape though.

- Put a lit candle inside one and leave it on the floor as a jack-o-
lantern/ monster-scarer, with every square lit by the candle given an
E-effect on certain monsters. The limited burn-time of candles should
keep this from being too powerful. For bonus points, have the game ask
the player which way the lantern is facing when he drops it, and
instead of a boring circle of light, illuminate a skull-face pattern
on the floor=3D-)

- Apply an amulet of magical breathing to one, give it a zap of undead
turning and you could #chat with it. "Yorick's skull laments that he
died so young," "dogscoff's skull warns you to watch out for [whatever
killed him]", "Xykon's skull complains of a headache" and so on. Maybe
a few spoilers about other potential skull-uses: "Roy's skull begs you
not to use him as a jack-o-lantern".

- Polyself appropriately and devour them for a little nutrition.
(Aren't giants supposed to 'grind bones to make bread'?)

- Grind a skull up (using a touchstone and/or blunt weapon?) and use
the resulting powder in alchemy. mix with water or oil or booze to
make... something. Foo healing, gain level or sickness could all be
justified, I suppose.

- Gift them to demon princes as part-payment on a bribe, or throw them
to pet demons to stop them betraying you (for a while at least)

- Players carrying skulls in open inventory could be refused entry to
delicatassens: "You can't bring that thing in here, it's unsanitary."
They don't seem to worry about people lugging half-rotted corpses
through their shops though...

- Apply a spear/javelin/quarterstaff to one to create a skull-on-a-
pole-sticking-out-the-ground thingy. Not sure what you'd use it for
mind you... Perhaps the skull's former owner (ie the bones ghost)
would be drawn to it rather rather than chasing after the player all
the time.

- Cavemen (and maybe orcs) could use skulls as a drums.

- Pile a skull on top of unihorns and/or worm teeth and polymorph it
into a skeleton named for the skull's owner. (Only one skull per pile
will go into each skeleton, to avoid name clashes). It would be cool
to resurrect, tame and ascend with a deceased character in this way.

- #offer a skull at an altar in order to (depending on class,
alignment, luck, deity's mood etc)
-- dispell or pacify the skull's ghost
-- put the deceased's soul to rest (to score alignment points and
godpoints, priests especially)
-- summon a demon (as per human sacrifice)
-- summon a sleepy-hollowesque headless horseman (OK, maybe that's a
bit much, although I like the idea of a tame, powerful, undead monster
that will turn on you the moment you accidentally drop/have stolen/
destroy its skull)
-- convert altar
-- convert self.

- There's also something tickling in my head concerning archeologists
prying gems from the eye-sockets of a skull, but it's a long time
since I watched the appropriate source material...

I'm sure other uses could be dreamed up. It's a shame the bones file
doesn't contain more data about the player, since a lot of the above
could be enhanced by knowing the alignment, class, race, geneder and
mode of death of the skull's owner. Then again, if you're breaking
bones compatibility by adding skulls, there's no harm in adding a
little background info to the skull when the bones are generated.


